## Phase 22b — Razorpay adapter, and the field-surface audit / 2026-09-02 / (this commit)

### Built
- `src/payments/`: `razorpayProvider.ts` (authorise/capture/void/refund behind
  Phase 15c's `PaymentProvider`), `razorpayOrders.ts`, `razorpayClient.ts`
  (OK/REFUSED/UNREACHABLE, swappable transport), `razorpaySignature.ts` (webhook
  HMAC + checkout handshake), `razorpayEvents.ts`, `gatewayState.ts` (the rank
  scale), `reconciliationDecision.ts` (pure), `razorpayPayouts.ts`,
  `selectProvider.ts`.
- `src/commit/`: `razorpayWebhook.ts`, `gatewayTransition.ts`,
  `paymentReconciliation.ts`, `payoutExecution.ts`, `technicianBoard.ts`,
  `technicianEarnings.ts`.
- Routes: `POST /webhooks/razorpay`, `POST /jobs/:id/checkout`,
  `POST /jobs/:id/checkout/callback`, `GET /vehicles`,
  `GET /technician/earnings`; `/technician/work` re-modelled.
- Tables: `gateway_events` (unique on `(provider, event_id)`),
  `payments.gateway_order_ref`, `payouts.provider_ref/failure_reason/sent_at`,
  `technicians.payout_fund_account_ref`. Ledger gains `platform:bank` and
  `payoutSettlementLines`.
- UI: driver page rebuilt (stepper, vehicle picker, hero card); technician page
  rebuilt (rung track, estimate, part-sourcing/destination context, release
  notice, earnings); dispatcher action queue; `tokens.css` remapped onto
  Bootstrap 5.3's `data-bs-theme`, split into console/field/driver/technician.
- Tests: `razorpayAdapter`, `razorpayWebhook`, `razorpayPayouts`,
  `concurrency/payments`. 854 fast + 12 concurrency, all green.

### Decisions
- Decision: our own `payments.gateway_order_ref` is the authority for whether an
  order exists, passed back in as `knownOrderRef`.
  Why: Stage 2 measured `GET /v1/orders?receipt=X` returning count 0 for an
  order created seconds earlier with that receipt.
  Alternative: trust the vendor's receipt filter. Why not: it opened a second
  order on every retry — one job, two checkout screens.
  Tradeoff: a lost write between creating an order and storing its ref orphans
  an unpaid order. Costs nothing (no money is held) and is not detectable.
- Decision: `AWAITING_CUSTOMER` added to `AuthoriseResult`; `refund` added as an
  OPTIONAL interface method.
  Why: Razorpay cannot hold money synchronously, and has no void endpoint — a
  captured payment is released only by refunding.
  Alternative: reuse UNREACHABLE. Why not: it means "we never got an answer",
  which is a different operational fact.
  Tradeoff: the interface grew. No business rule moved — Phase 15c's suite
  passes unchanged, which is the check that matters.
- Decision: the reconciliation sweep, not the webhook, is the correctness
  mechanism. See `reconciliationDecision.ts`.
- Decision: technician-facing wording is a server-side whitelist
  (`technicianUpdates.ts`), mirroring `driverUpdates.ts`.
  Why: the technician page was rendering the dispatcher's raw audit panel.
  Alternative: a formatter with a `default:` branch. Why not: the failure mode
  of forgetting becomes SCREAMING_CASE on a phone.

### Concurrency
- Scenario: a driver taps Pay now while the dispatch saga is still committing.
  What races: `authoriseCallout` from `POST /jobs/:id/checkout` and from
  `authoriseCalloutQuietly`, both read-then-create on `payments`.
  Why dangerous: the loser died on `idempotency_key` and returned a 500 for a
  payment that had in fact been created correctly.
  How handled: catch the unique violation and re-read the winner's row — the
  shape `postTransaction` already used. Prisma's `upsert` was tried FIRST and
  rejected by the new test: it compiles to select-then-insert, not
  `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT`, so three concurrent callers still collided.
  Assumption relied on: the unique index on `payments.idempotency_key`.
- Scenario: Razorpay redelivers a webhook. Handled by inserting the
  `gateway_events` row in the same transaction as the transition it causes.
- Scenario: `payment.captured` arrives before `payment.authorized`. Handled by
  `movesForward` — a transition applies only if it moves the payment forward.

### Weaknesses introduced
- Payout execution is verified against a SCRIPTED rail only. RazorpayX
  credentials reach the API (`GET /v1/payouts` returns 200 with the configured
  account number) but no payout has been sent. Matters: yes, at any real scale.
- `payment_capture: false` is UNVERIFIED against a completed checkout. If the
  account auto-captures, the callout fee is taken at checkout and there is
  nothing left to capture on completion — the two-stage split becomes fiction.
  Matters: yes. It is the single largest untested assumption in the phase.
- A 401 from Razorpay still surfaces to callers as UNREACHABLE. It is now
  logged distinctly, but the retry sweep cannot tell a credential problem from
  an outage and will keep retrying. Does not matter at current scale.
- The reconciliation sweep is unbounded per pass beyond `SWEEP_LIMIT = 25`; a
  long outage clears at 25 payments per five minutes.
- The dispatcher readability pass is PARTIAL — see below.

### What broke
- The audit's own finding: the previous UI round was DRIVER-PAGE-ONLY. The
  dispatcher got three lines (`data-bs-theme`, a stylesheet link). Reported
  rather than quietly fixed.
- `GET /v1/orders?receipt=` does not filter. Found on the first live call.
- The payment race above — found by clicking Pay now on a real page, not by
  the fast suite, which calls `authoriseCallout` once.
- Bootstrap 5.3's `.btn-outline-*` emits a literal `color`, so remapping
  `--bs-btn-color` left outline buttons blue while the variable read amber.
  Only a screenshot caught it.
- `MARUTI_SUZUKI` reached both the driver's vehicle picker and the technician's
  job card. Now `src/domain/vehicleWords.ts`, shared.
- `data-panel="job-events"` on the technician page rendered `jobDetail.js` —
  the dispatcher's raw JSON audit panel — on a field phone.

### Concepts now genuinely in use
HMAC request signing over a raw body; vendor idempotency (three different
mechanisms in one integration); the dual-write problem and convergent
reconciliation as its only real answer; a monotonic state rank for out-of-order
message delivery; Bootstrap 5.3 colour modes; WCAG 2.2 target sizing.

### Interview angle
"Your payment gateway returns success and your database write is lost. What
happens?" — answerable with the reconciliation worker and, more importantly,
with why a webhook is not sufficient. And: "how did you find the concurrency bug
in your own payment path?" — by adding a second caller and clicking the button.
